%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                          Introduction                         %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% TODO: do I want this?
(content warnings)
	(div @meta) {
		(span @bold) { Content warning: }
		This game includes significant \( but non-graphic \) discussion of murder and capital punishment, mentions of historical \( first millennium BCE \) ableism, and minor depiction of psychedelics used for ritual purposes. The author considers it “PG” rated: not unsuitable for children, but parental guidance is suggested. (par)
		(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
			Click below or press ENTER to continue.
		(else)
			Press any key to continue.
		(endif)
	}
	(pause)
	(clear all)

(intro)
	(content warnings)
	(div @initial-spacer) (space)
	Six years of work, countless hours in the library and the lab, hunched over your laptop, poring over old manuscripts or departmental paperwork...and now it’s almost over. Your advisor has given you a deadline, and you need to defend your thesis by the end of the year, or leave with nothing to show for it. (par)
	Your data is promising. The experimental results are fantastic, the p-values are significant, and you’ve gotten a couple publications out of the process. But if you want to be the first ever PhD in Forensic Necromancy, you need more than lab results. You need something the world can’t ignore. (par)
	Which means you need human remains in situ, and the older the better. The last of your advisor’s grant funding got you to Nineveh just after midnight. The rest is up to you.
	(banner)
	(try [look])

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                            Tutorial                           %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% Give people a way to turn it off, but it starts enabled
(tutorial is active)
(understand command [tutorial off])
(perform [tutorial off])
	(now) ~(tutorial is active)
	(div @meta) { Tutorial messages will no longer be shown. }
(understand command [tutorial on])
(perform [tutorial on])
	(now) (tutorial is active)
	(div @meta) { Tutorial messages will now be shown at various points. }
(give the tutorial) ~(tutorial is active)
(option paragraph)
	(if) (tutorial is active) (then)
		Tutorial messages will appear at various points. Use (command link {TUTORIAL OFF}) to change this.
	(else)
		Tutorial messages will not appear. Use (command link {TUTORIAL ON}) to change this.
	(endif)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught suggestions)
	(suggestions enabled)
	(current focus $)
	(div @tutorial) {
		After you examine something, the bar at the bottom of the screen will suggest some actions you might do with it.
	}
	(now) (taught suggestions)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught secondary objects)
	(current focus $Focus)
	($Focus has secondary objects) %% Things that mention nouns in their description
	(div @tutorial) {
		Sometimes an object will only be mentioned in the description of another object. But you can still
		(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
			click
		(else)
			interact with
		(endif)
		them, just like nouns in the main description.
	}
	(now) (taught secondary objects)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught rope)
	(current focus #rope)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Examining things will often suggest what you might do with them. The rope, for example, is meant to be climbed. But where would that be useful?
	}
	(now) (taught rope)

(give the tutorial) %% Teaches you about moving objects in dreams
	~(taught spear)
	(current focus #spear)
	(div @tutorial) {
		You can’t change the past, but all of this is happening inside your head. If you pick things up and move them from one memory to another, they’ll act the way your brain expects.
	}
	(now) (taught spear)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught moving)
	(#camp is unvisited) %% These are the two rooms adjacent to the start
	(#abovedig is unvisited)
	(div @tutorial) {
		This game is played by
		(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
			clicking links or
		(endif)
		typing in commands. To move around, for example,
		(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
			click or
		(endif)
		type a compass direction: (command link {southwest}) \( (command link {sw}) \) or (command link {south}) \( (command link {s}) \) .
	}
	(now) (taught moving)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught examining)
	(current room $Room)
	*($Obj has ancestor $Room)
	~(current player $Obj)
	~(held $Obj)
	(div @tutorial) {
		(color link [links] {Highlighted}) words indicate things you can interact with.
		(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
			Click or type
		(else)
			Type
		(endif)
		these words to examine them.
	}
	(now) (taught examining)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught items)
	~(taught inventory)
	(current focus $Obj)
	(item $Obj)
	(div @tutorial) {
		When you see a small object, like (the $Obj), you can pick it up and take it with you.
	}
	(now) (taught items)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught interaction)
	(current focus #backpack)
	(suggestions enabled)
	(div @tutorial) {
		After you examine something, the bar above the command prompt suggests some possible commands you could try.
		(if) (#backpack is open) (then)
			If something is open, for example, you might close it;
		(else)
			If something is closed, for example, you might open it;
		(endif)
		(if) (#backpack is #wornby $) (then)
			if you’re wearing something, you might take it off.
		(else)
			if something is wearable, you might put it on.
		(endif)
		But you can always ignore it and type your own commands instead.
	}
	(now) (taught interaction)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught inventory)
	*($Obj is #heldby #p-real)
	~($Obj = #tools)
	(div @tutorial) {
		A lot of text adventures involve finding objects and moving them around. To see what you’re currently holding, (command link {take inventory})
		(if) ~(interpreter supports links) (then)
			\( or (command link {i}) for short \)
		(endif)
		.
	}
	(now) (taught inventory)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught map)
	(map enabled)
	(#camp is visited) %% At least three visited rooms on the map
	(#abovedig is visited) %% You need to specifically visit these two (plus the starting room) before you can get anywhere else
	(div @tutorial) {
		The map
		(if) (interpreter supports status bar) (then)
			at the top of the screen
		(else)
			above the command prompt
		(endif)
		marks your current location with a (span @command) \@
		(if) (pointers enabled) (then)
			, and any puzzles you’re currently able to solve with a (space) (span @command) !
		(endif)
		.
		(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
			Click any room to go there.
		(endif)
	}
	(now) (taught map)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught puzzles)
	(movement has been prevented) %% Tried to go down the pit
	(#dig is unvisited)
	(div @tutorial) {
		You can try all sorts of actions in this game, but that doesn’t mean they’ll always be effective or useful. When something doesn’t work, pay attention to the reason why: it might suggest a different approach.
	}
	(now) (taught puzzles)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught nudges)
	{ (movement has been prevented) (or) (#dig is visited) }
	(div @tutorial) {
		If you’re ever not sure where to go, (command link {NUDGE}) will tell you what obstacles you can currently make progress on.
		(if) (pointers enabled) (then)
			You’ll also see them as (space) (span @command) ? signs on the map.
		(elseif) (map enabled) (then)
			You can also use (command link {POINTERS ON}) to see them marked on your map.
		(endif)
	}
	(now) (taught nudges)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught options)
	(examined something)
	(div @tutorial) {
		If this interface isn’t working for you, you can adjust it in the (command link {OPTIONS}).
		(backend-specific options tutorial)
	}
	(now) (taught options)
(backend-specific options tutorial) %% Don’t fail

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught references)
	(current room #n-announcement)
	(div @tutorial) {
		When you come across unfamiliar names and terms, you may want to look them up in the encyclopedia. Remember that you can move between the real world and the dream world at any time: all you have to do is ({wake up}).
	}
	(now) (taught references)

(give the tutorial) %% Don’t fail

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                     The Real World : Above                    %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

#nabiyunus
(room *)
(* has coordinates [2 2 1])
(name *) Tell Nabi Yunus
(proper *)
(look *)
	No hiking through the Iraqi desert for you: the dig site is right in the heart of Mosul, the mound known as Prophet Jonah’s Hill. There’s supposed to be a very nice bakery across the street, but it wasn’t open at 2 in the morning; you had to make do with a box of cookies from the supermarket on the way over. (par)
	But you made it.
	(if) (#dig is unvisited) (then)
		They say the field crew came across more bones today, and that’s exactly what you need.
	(elseif) ~(finish command unlocked) (then)
		You found the human remains you’re looking for; now all you need is enough data to cap off your thesis.
	(else)
		You’ve found all the data you’re looking for. Now you can finish up whenever you’re ready.
	(endif)

(from * go #south to #abovedig)
(from * go #southwest to #camp)

#tools
(name *) various tools
(dict *) tool ball compass mirror entheogen entheogens tablet tablets pill pills keychain packet
(plural *)
(your *)
(item *)
(* is #heldby #p-real)
(descr *)
	The tools of a forensic necromancer’s trade: a mirror, a packet of entheogens, and a keychain with a little ball compass. Everything you need to divine memories of the past. (par)
	Well, maybe the compass isn’t strictly necessary. But archaeologists lay everything out by compass directions, so that one helps you find those memories in the first place.

(prevent [drop/put * | $])
	But without your tools, how would you accomplish anything here?

(prevent [take *]) %% Needs to handle both TAKE COMPASS and TAKE PILLS
	You’ve got them all close at hand, ready to use when the moment comes.
	(if) (current room $Room) ($Room is one of [#dig #looseearth #deeppit]) (then)
		All you have to do now is (link) {divine the bones} .
	(endif)

#camp
(room *)
(name *) camp
(your *)
(room header *) Your camp
(look *)
	The main field crew keeps their equipment off to the (#south), but you’ve made an unobtrusive little camp here
	(if) (#backpack is pristine) (then)
		: your (#bike) locked to a piece of machinery, your (#backpack) looped over the handlebars.
	(else)
		, with your (#bike) locked to a convenient piece of ({machinery}) .
	(endif)

(from * go #south to #realcamp) ~(real camp explained)
(from * go #northeast to #nabiyunus)
(from * go #east to #abovedig)

#bike
(name *) bicycle
(dict *) bike handlebar handlebars handle bar bars handles
(your *)
(* is #in #camp)
(descr *) It’s faster than walking, and more reliable than a cab in the early hours of the morning.
(on-seat *)
(lockable *)
~(openable *)
(* is locked)

(prevent [enter/climb/unlock/open/untie * | $]) (bike refusal)

(grammar [ride [single]] for [climb $]) %% In case

#machinery
(name *) piece of machinery
(dict *) machine lock
(* is #in #camp)
(descr *) You have no idea what this machine does, but for right now it’s large and immovable.

(prevent [unlock/open/untie * | $]) (bike refusal)

(bike refusal)
	You’re not ready to leave yet, and you’re also not ready to bring a bike any closer to a delicate archaeological site.
	(if) (finish command unlocked) (then)
		(div @meta) {
			If you (emph {are}) ready to leave, use the (command link {FINISH}) command to end the game.
		}
	(endif)

#backpack
(name *) backpack
(your *)
(descr *)
	Very large and very sturdy
	(if) (* is open) (then)
		(collect $Item)
			*($Item is #in *)
		(into $List)
		. (A $List) (is $List) packed inside.
	(else)
		, and currently zipped up tight.
	(endif)
(* has secondary objects) (* is open) ($ is #in *)
(container *)
(openable *)
(wearable *)
(* is #on #bike)

(appearance $ #in *) %% Let the description handle the appearances

(after [inventory])
	(held *)
	(if) (* is open) (then)
		(collect $Item)
			*($Item is #in *)
		(into $List)
		(par) (A $List) (is $List) in the backpack.
	(else)
		(par) The backpack is closed.
	(endif)

(after [open *])
	(now) (backpack opened)
(puzzle *)
(solved *) (backpack opened)

#waterbottle %% Not useful, but shows that you can’t carry things into dreams
(name *) water bottle
(item *)
(* is #in #backpack)
(potable *)
(descr *) Even if you’re not trekking through the desert, the temperature in Mosul is well over 30 degrees at night. Passing out from dehydration won’t get your thesis finished.

(instead of [open/empty *]) (try [drink *])
(prevent [fill * | $]) There’s nothing else around to fill it from. Better make it last.
(perform [drink *]) You take a judiciously-measured drink. Compared to the air around you, it’s still nice and cold.

%% Book and rope are also in backpack: see systems.dg

#abovedig
(name *) above the dig site
(dict *) origin
(heads *) above
(room *)
(look *) From this upper rim you can see the whole excavation spread out below you, marked out with ({string}) into a chessboard of squares. A ({metal post}) marks the origin point of the whole thing.

%% (from * go #south to #realcamp) ~(real camp explained)
(from * go #north to #nabiyunus)
(from * go #west to #camp)
(from * go #east through #rope-u to #dig) (upper rope connected)
(from * go #east to #dig) ~(upper rope connected) %% Prevented
(from * go #down to #east)

(instead of [jump])
	(current room *)
	\( off the edge \) (line)
	(try [go #down])

(prevent [leave * #east])
	~(upper rope connected)
	~{ (#cart is #in #dig) (#cart is locked) }
	This ledge is about three meters above the dig site—farther than you’re willing to jump. You’ll need some way to climb down.
	(now) (movement has been prevented)

(narrate leaving * #east)
	(#cart is #in #dig)
	(#cart is locked)
	With the cart under the ledge, there’s barely any drop at all. You hop down onto the cart, then onto the ground.

#post
(name *) survey post
(dict *) metal datum
(suggest pushing *)
(* is #in #abovedig)
(descr *) This metal post marks the datum: the origin of the archaeologists’ coordinate system. It’s anchored very securely into the ground—if it moved even a few centimeters, after all, every single measurement would need to be redone.

#grid
(name *) grid of string
(dict *) strings square squares nail nails
(suggest pushing *)
(descr *) Nails are set into the ground every ten meters, north and south and east and west \( and in some cases up and down \). A grid of strings between them divides the whole site into squares.

(#abovedig/#dig/#looseearth/#deeppit attracts *)

(perform [push/pull #post/#grid])
	The strings aren’t very strong, but the datum post is solid—you yank on it with all your strength and it doesn’t budge.

#realcamp
(name *) official camp
(room *)
(look *) You should never be here! Please report this bug!
(never accessible *)

(prevent entering *)
	Better not. It’s not like you’re a looter, or you’re not supposed to be here, or something like that...you have all the right paperwork, you’re officially a guest of the archaeologists, and besides, it’s not like you’re going to be (emph {taking}) anything. It’s just so much easier to do these things in the early hours of the morning, when you don’t have to explain what a forensic necromancer is, or why you need silence to meditate over the bones.
	(now) (real camp explained) %% Remove the map connection

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                     The Real World : Below                    %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

#dig
(name *) excavation
(dict *) dig site 0n10e 0n 10e
(heads *) dig site
(room *)
(look *)
	Or as the archaeologists call it, 0N10E: zero meters north and ten meters east of the datum. This square has been excavated more than its neighbors, sinking it a few meters into the earth.

(after [look]) %% Move to the very bottom of the room description
	(current room *)
	(if) (eastern connection unlocked) (#looseearth is unvisited) (then)
		(par) It’s hard to keep your bearings between the real world and a memory, but those other remains should be somewhere to the ({east}). Hopefully some of them are still there.
	(endif)
	(if) (northern connection unlocked) (#deeppit is unvisited) (then)
		(par) If those other bones were to the east, then the secret passage should be...due north.
	(endif)

(from * go #east to #looseearth) (eastern connection unlocked)
(from * go #north to #deeppit) (northern connection unlocked)
(from * go #west through #rope-u to #abovedig) (upper rope connected)
(from * go #west to #abovedig) ~(upper rope connected) %% Prevented
(from * go #up to #west)

%% The following is an *incredibly* hacky way to avoid the (recursively leave non-vehicles) check that happens (before $) going anywhere
%% We circumvent those (before $) checks altogether when redirecting to another action
%% THIS IS A BAD IDEA! DO NOT IMITATE THIS IN YOUR OWN CODE!

(try [go #west/#up])
	(current room *)
	(maybe jump from cart)
(try [leave * #west/#up])
	(current room *)
	(maybe jump from cart)

%% Try using the cart if it’s available
(maybe jump from cart)
	(#cart is #in *)
	(if) ~{ (current player $Player) ($Player is #on #cart) } (then)
		(first try [climb #cart])
	(endif)
	(try [jump])

(perform [jump])
	(current room *)
	(current player $Player)
	($Player is #on #cart)
	Standing on the cart, you have just enough height to plant your arms firmly on top of the bank. One little \( or maybe moderate \) leap and you’re hauling yourself up.
	(move player to #in #abovedig)

(prevent [leave * #west])
	~(upper rope connected)
	(current player $Player)
	~($Player is #on #cart)
	The datum is about three meters above the dig site, and the slope is steep. You’ll need some way to climb up.

%% There are many bones in this game
(name (bones $)) bone fragments
(dict (bones $)) bones fragment remains
(plural *(bones $))
(clarify location of *(bones $))
(when (bones $) is out of reach)
	Probably best not to touch the artifacts. Even if you’re an official guest of the dig team, you’re not a trained archaeologist, and there’s always the risk of contaminating something in the process.

#bones-n
(bones *)
(dict *) of nabuu-ahu-eresh nabuu ahu eresh
(* is #in #dig)
(* divines to #n-ziqqurat)
(descr *)
	There’s not much, just a few white glints in the moonlight
	(if) (#n-execution is unvisited) (then)
		...or is it just your imagination? There’s only one way to be sure: put your skills to the test and (link [divine the bones]) {divine them} .
	(else)
		: the remains of Nabuu-ahu-eresh of Babylon.
	(endif)
(appearance *)
	(if) (#n-ziqqurat is unvisited) (then)
		According to the dig reports, these are the coordinates where human remains were found earlier today. With any luck, there should be some ({bone fragments}) left to find.
	(else)
		You can just barely make out some ({bone fragments}) in the moonlight.
	(endif)

(puzzle *)
(solved *) (#n-ziqqurat is visited)

(narrate divining *)
	%% Always the first bones you divine, probably also the first bones you return to
	(select)
		This is it. You sit down and get as comfortable as you can, making sure the bones are clearly in your field of view. The mirror goes in front of your left eye, adjusted until the images overlap: one eye seeing the bones, the other reflected back at itself. The entheogen tablet under your tongue, where it starts to dissolve. Now, to meditate. (par)
		The idea of a memory palace goes back to the ancient Greeks: anchoring your memories by laying them out in an imaginary space. Forensic necromancy is the same. You just do it for someone else’s memories instead. Drawing them from the bones, guiding them, giving them shape. Until lines start to shine in your mind, and colors, and...
	(or)
		The second time is always easier than the first. You know the shape of the memory palace already, and you can use it to guide yourself back. Pull out the mirror, slip another tablet under your tongue, and imagine the outlines of the ziggurat, overlaid on the site...
	(or)
		With practiced ease, you sit down next to the bone fragments and will yourself back into memory.
	(stopping)

#looseearth
(name *) loose earth
(dict *) 0n20e 0n 20e
(room *)
(look *) The square at 0N20E has been less thoroughly excavated than 0N10E, and the earth is softer here: the archaeologists have been working their way through it, breaking up each layer and sifting through the dirt for artifacts.

(from * go #west to #dig)
(from * go #northwest to #deeppit) (#deeppit is visited) %% Cleaner to only show the north-south connection on the map until it’s been explored, but after that it’s fine to allow NW-SE movement also

#finds
(name *) latest finds
(dict *) artifact artifacts find
(plural *)
(item *)
(* is #in #looseearth)
(descr *) A ({broken tablet}), a ({hunk of metal})...and a handful of ({white fragments}) off to the side.
(when * is out of reach) Probably best not to touch the artifacts. Even if you’re an official guest of the dig team, you’re not a trained archaeologist, and there’s always the risk of contaminating something in the process.
(appearance *)
	The ({latest finds}) have been carefully sorted and labelled in trays off to one side
	(if) (#s-palace is visited) (then)
		(no space) —including the bones of Sharhiya of Babylon
	(endif)
	.

#tablet
(name *) broken tablet
(dict *) clay
(* is #partof #finds)
(descr *) About the size of your thumbnail, with a few cuneiform signs visible on the top face. (span @bold) Namzaqu : something about a “key”?

#hunk
(name *) hunk of metal
(dict *) iron
(* is #partof #finds)
(descr *) Probably iron, in this time period, but in this state all you can do is guess.

#bones-s
(bones *)
(dict *) of sharhiya sharhiya’s sharhiya's
(* is #partof #finds)
(* divines to #s-palace)
(descr *)
	Just a few white fragments among the artifacts—it’s hard to even tell which bones they are.
	(if) (#s-palace is unvisited) (then)
		Could they belong to the Sharhiya from the memories?
	(else)
		But they’re certainly Sharhiya’s.
	(endif)

(puzzle *)
(solved *) (#s-palace is visited)

(narrate divining *)
	(select)
		You have to try a couple positions before you find one that works: you need a clear view of the bones, but if you’re too vertical, you might fall over when the visions set in. Eventually you settle down on the wall between the grid squares and pull out your mirror and tablets. (par)
		This one is easier than the first. You already have some memories from the same time and place; you hold your focus on those, using them as an anchor for the new visions. Soon the outlines start to take form...
	(or)
		You sit back on the wall, pulling out your mirror and slipping easily back into the memories.
	(stopping)

%% Cart is also here, see systems.dg

#deeppit
(name *) deep pit
(dict *) n10e10 n10 e10
(room *)
(* has coordinates [3 2 1])
(look *) N10E10 is only half a square: a five-by-ten rectangle angled sharply downward until it intersects some kind of underground ({cavity}). The other half of the square is nothing but a yawning pit, extending under the ground to the north.

(from * go #south to #dig)
(from * go #southeast to #looseearth)
(from * go #down to #north)
(from * go #north through #rope-d to #tunnel) (lower rope connected)
(from * go #north to #tunnel) ~(lower rope connected) %% Blocked

(prevent [leave * #north])
	~(lower rope connected)
	You can’t see much in the moonlight, but the pit must be at least a few meters deep—farther than you’re willing to drop without something to hold onto.

#cavity
(name *) opening in the ground
(dict *) hole cavity dark deep
(* is #in #deeppit)
(actor container *)
(descr *) It doesn’t look like it was dug by the archaeologists—it doesn’t follow the grid, and there’s a good meter and a half of solid dirt above it. And the ({electrical wires}) are clearly improvised.

(puzzle *)
(* requires #cart)
(solved *)
	(lower rope connected) (or) (#tunnel is visited)

(perform [enter *])
	(try [go #down])
(perform [put $Obj #in *])
	You toss (the $Obj) into the pit, and it lands with a muffled
	(select)
		(emph {thump})
	(or)
		(emph {whump})
	(or)
		(emph {thud})
	(at random)
	.

%% Don’t let them throw the rope in or they won’t be able to get it back
%% Anything else can be recovered by means of the cart and rope
(prevent [put #rope #in *])
	Tossing the rope into the pit is a fine idea, but it should be tied to something first. Otherwise, how will you get it back?

#wires
(name *) electrical wires
(plural *)
(dict *) electric cable wire cables
(* is #partof #cavity)
(descr *)
	These ones do follow the grid as they snake down into the pit
	(if) (#tunnel is visited) (then)
		, powering the lights down below.
	(else)
		. Maybe there are lights set up down there?
	(endif)

#tunnel
(name *) looters’ tunnel
(dict *) looter looter's looter’s looters'
(room *)
(look *) The archaeologists are working through the site layer by layer, but looters are less meticulous: the fastest way to get at the artifacts is to tunnel in from the side. This one is narrow, about four feet across, and runs approximately northwest to southeast.

(from * go #south through #rope-d to #deeppit) (lower rope connected)
