	(endif)

%% Vignette 3
#n-argument
(vignette *)

(narrate vignette for *)
	“Absolutely not.” You glare across the table at your brother. The single lamp gives his face a strange, uncanny cast. “We have everything to lose, and (emph {nothing}) to gain.” (par)
	“Except for saving someone’s life. Saving the (emph {king’s}) life!” (par)
	“He wasn’t crowned by Marduk. He’s no true king. And have you forgotten what he did to Babylon? Let the Assyrians all stab each other in the back. What happens up in Nineveh isn’t our concern.” (par)
	“If some Babylonians save the king’s life, maybe that will change.” He searches your face, eyes gleaming, and sighs at what he sees. “And besides. The crown prince is different. Asshur-ahu-iddina spends time around here, he fixes things that need fixing. Urad-Mullissi is just another Assyrian. If he seizes the throne, this continues on for a generation more. If we stop this, if Asshur-ahu-iddina inherits, we’ll have someone reasonable in power. Someone who will get Marduk’s blessing and rebuild the temples.” (par)
	You close your eyes for a long moment. “Fine. Make the trip, make your report. I’ll take care of things back here. But if you expect gratitude from the Assyrians...” (par)
	You meet his gaze and hold it as the shadows start to close in. “You’re going to be disappointed.” (par)
	And the light gutters out.

(protagonist description for *)
	You’re the same tall man in a Babylonian tunic, though your clothes are simple and comfortable for lounging about the house.

(name *) back room
