%% Avoid shenanigans once it’s lit
(fire source *) (lamp lit)
(prevent [pour/empty * | $])
	(lamp lit)
	Tipping the lamp over while it’s still burning is a good way to get hot oil all over your hands.
(instead of [fill $Obj with *])
	(lamp lit)
	(try [pour * into $Obj])
(prevent [douse *])
	(lamp lit)
	After all the trouble it took to get this lit? Better not.
(instead of [pour $ into *])
	(lamp lit)
	(try [douse *])

(perform [burn *])
	~(* is filled with #oil)
	(fire source $Source is visible)
	You hold the lamp against (the $Source) for a moment, but the wick just chars. It needs to be filled with oil to burn properly.
	(if) (* is filled with #water) (then)
		\( The water definitely isn’t helping, either. \)
	(endif)
	(tick) (stop)
(perform [burn *])
	(fire source $Source is visible)
	You hold the lamp against (the $Source) for a moment, making sure there’s enough oil on the wick, and when you pull it away it’s shining with a small but steady yellow flame.
	(now) (lamp lit)

(prevent [switch * | $])
	It’s not the kind of lamp that you switch. It’s the ancient sort that you fill with oil and ignite.

