%% Puzzle room blocking Vignette 3
#s-throneroom
(name *) cramped passage
(room *)
(look *) While the jumble of room-fragments to the west feels larger than any building could be, this one feels tight and confined, no matter how big the fragments are. Pieces of memory fit together at odd angles and thick, sticky darkness coats every surface, flowing back into place as soon as your lamp moves past. Off to the northeast, though, you can make out a flicker of movement.

(from * go #west to #s-palace)
(from * go #northeast to #s-revelation)

(prevent entering *)
	~(sufficient light in *)
	You take a few steps into the darkness, but suddenly it closes in around you, thick on your skin and suffocating in your mouth! You stumble back toward the light before it can tighten any further.

(narrate leaving #s-palace #east) You take a deep breath and step into the darkness. It flows away from your lamp like some kind of viscous fluid, leaving a small patch of visibility around you.
(narrate leaving * #west) You step back into the light.

%% Vignette 3
#s-revelation
(vignette *)

(narrate vignette for *)
	You don’t dare take the blindfold off, or run, or anything drastic—so you’re left frozen there while the people around you discuss your fate. You were betrayed. Who else could the man on the throne be except Urad-Mullissi? (par)
	The sound of your name pierces through your thoughts. “Sharhiya of Babylon, was it?” \( You try your best to nod. \) “You can take that off now. The little charade’s served its purpose.” (par)
	As your eyes come into focus, you can see him directly now: tall and broad, built like a wrestler, with fierce eyes and a thick beard. He waits patiently for you to fumble your cloak back on. (par)
	“Now, Sharhiya of Babylon, this is very important. I need you to tell me exactly what you heard, and who you heard it from. Tzillaya, please get this verbatim.” The stout man nods and pulls out a new stylus. Then all three of them look at you expectantly while you stammer and grasp for the words. (par)
	Eventually, Urad-Mullissi sighs and stands up from the throne. “I swear to Asshur that you will not be punished for anything you speak of today. Nabiya, Tzillaya, make sure he’s properly compensated, with a little extra for lodgings until he returns to Babylon. But I need to know every detail, exactly as you remember it. (par)
	“You see,” he steps toward you, and you have to fight the urge to back away, “my brother has been planning something, he and his mother. He doesn’t know how to quit while he’s ahead. Father’s gotten suspicious. And now he’s overplayed his hand—now, I have proof that he’s trying to frame me for treason. This testimony should be enough to get him exiled, or worse.” (par)
	The prince sees your flinch, and stops moving closer. “So I would consider it a great favor, to me and to my father, if you’d testify under oath. I know the royal family is...not popular in Babylon, for very good reason. But avoiding a civil war would be better for us all. And...perhaps when I take the throne, I can try to mend some of those rifts.” (par)
	So you think back, and start to recount your story...

(protagonist description for *)
	You’re the short, rotund Babylonian again, sweating through your finest clothes after discovering that it was Urad-Mullissi on the throne.

(name *) audience chamber
