	The new memory transitions smoothly into the old ones, one more hole in the borders of the ziggurat. It’s starting to look like swiss cheese around the edges.

#cons-lines
(name *) surveying lines
(dict *) survey line walls wall cord cords rope ropes peg pegs
(plural *)
(* is #in #m-unfinished)
(descr *) These surveying lines don’t look too different from the cords the modern archaeologists used to lay out their grid. Even thousands of years later, the best way to make a straight line is still to stretch a rope between a couple of pegs. Instead of a regular grid, though, these ones seem to be tracing the original walls, making sure the new ones follow exactly the same outline.

#cons-bricks
(name *) piles of bricks
(dict *) pile brick old new
(plural *)
(* is #in #m-unfinished)
(descr *)
	Stacks and stacks of new bricks, fresh out of the kiln, their edges still perfectly sharp. A short inscription is stamped on each one in Assyrian: “I am Asshur-ahu-iddina, King of Assyria, Viceroy of Babylon, beloved of Asshur, beloved of Marduk.”
	(unlock #t-asshur-ahu-iddina)
	(unlock #t-gods)

%% Vignette 1, automatically unlocked
#m-epilogue
(vignette *)

(narrate vignette for *)
	“The construction is almost finished, Your Majesty.” You keep your nose pressed to the floor as you hear the king move; he’s not the sort to tolerate insubordination. “We thank you again for your generosity. We greatly appreciate it—and so does Marduk. Once the temple is fully rebuilt, he is certain to bless your reign.” (par)
	“Certain?” The king’s voice is sharp and accusing, and you try not to wince. “How can you be certain when it comes to the gods?” (par)
	Better play it safe. “How could anyone, divine or mortal, not appreciate such a gift as Your Majesty has given? How could anyone in Babylon doubt your kingship after the wisdom and kindness you have shown? The four corners of the world bow to you, Your Majesty, as well they should.” (par)
	He sniffs and turns away, and you relax just a fraction. “Very well. Finish the construction. I want to see the cult statue placed before the end of the year. I need Marduk’s blessing as soon as possible. (emph {Need}) it. Do you understand? And also, I need a full review of the temple staff. My mother’s diviners say there are traitors there, and I need to root them out. Purging...let’s say two thirds of the staff should do it.” (par)
	“Two thirds?!” You sit up sharply before you can stop yourself. “Your Majesty! The Temple of Marduk is loyal to you! We need—” (par)
	Suddenly the king is very, very close to you, his eyes blazing with fury. “Is it you, then? You’re the one she warned me about? It must be. It has to be. Yes. That’s why—that’s why it—” (par)
	“Your Majesty! Please! My loyalty has never been in question! I orchestrated that ‘conspiracy’ for you; without me, you wouldn’t—” (par)
	It was the wrong thing to say. In a split second the king’s hands are on your throat, with far more strength than you would have expected from his frail body. “I. Need. (emph {No one}). I can trust. (emph {No one}). It was you...it was you, all along! And once I’m rid of you...then, then, that’s when I’ll finally be safe!”

(protagonist description for *) Lean and round-shouldered, with wispy gray hair: this must be the High Priest of Marduk that the brothers reported to.

(name *) hidden chamber
(look *)
	(select)
		\( You extricate yourself from the king’s grip and stand up, brushing yourself off. That was unpleasant. \) (par)
	(or)
	(stopping)
	A small, windowless room, tight and low-ceilinged, hidden somewhere behind the palace walls. The gap to the south looks like an intrusion from a different memory; without it, you can’t see any trace of an entrance or exit. (par)
	The only other occupant is ({a frail man}) in resplendent clothes, his face contorted with rage.

#asshiya
(name *) frail man
(dict *) king asshur-ahu-iddina resplendent clothes face
(male *)
(* is #in #m-epilogue)
(descr *) He doesn’t seem at all well—he looks thin and weak despite a royal diet, and a livid red rash spreads from the bridge of his nose across each cheek. Before modern medicine, that would have been taken as either a curse or a sign of divine disfavor. Neither is a good look for a king.

